---
/**
 * /systems/menu-platform — the payments case study.
 *
 * Same template, same slots, same order as the other two. This file supplies
 * prose and diagrams only.
 */
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import MenuArchitecture from '../../components/diagrams/MenuArchitecture.astro';
import MenuPaymentFlow from '../../components/diagrams/MenuPaymentFlow.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('systems', 'menu-platform');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing content entry: systems/menu-platform');

const SANITISATION =
  'Demo or staging tenant only. No real restaurant name, logo, address, or phone number in ' +
  'frame; no real payment identifiers; browser chrome cropped or a clean profile with no ' +
  'bookmarks, extensions, or console visible.';

const PENDING_GALLERY = [
  'Screenshot 1 of 4 — the public menu as a diner sees it after scanning, mobile 390x844, with the category navigation and a few items visible.',
  'Screenshot 2 of 4 — the restaurant dashboard menu editor, desktop 1440x900, showing categories and items.',
  'Screenshot 3 of 4 — the QR card generator with a theme selected, desktop 1440x900. Use a demo tenant code, not a live one.',
  'Screenshot 4 of 4 — the platform console tenant list, desktop 1440x900. Every tenant name and contact detail must be demo data.',
];

const ownerInputMarkers = PENDING_GALLERY.map(
  (asset) => `<!-- OWNER-INPUT: ${asset} ${SANITISATION} -->`,
).join('\n');
---

<CaseStudyLayout entry={entry}>
  <Fragment slot="context">
    <p>
      A restaurant puts a small printed code on each table. A diner scans it and the menu opens — no
      app, no account, no waiting for a laminated card to come back round. That is the whole product
      from the outside, and it is deliberately unremarkable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The part that is not unremarkable is what sits behind it. This is a live commercial platform:
      restaurants subscribe, pay, upgrade, downgrade, lapse, and renew, and every one of those
      transitions is code I wrote and carry. A trial that quietly fails to convert, or a payment
      that takes money without activating anything, is not a bug report — it is someone's money and
      someone's evening service.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three surfaces, one process: the public menu a diner scans into, the dashboard a restaurant
      signs into, and the console I operate the platform from.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="constraints">
    <p>
      <strong>Real money, and no one to escalate to.</strong> Payment bugs are not deferred to a payments
      team, because there isn't one. The failure modes had to be designed for rather than discovered.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Diners are not customers.</strong> The person scanning the code has no relationship with
      the subscription and never agreed to anything. Nothing about the plan a restaurant is on should
      ever be their problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A gateway that confirms twice.</strong> Payment confirmation arrives by two independent
      routes that can land in either order, out of order, or not at all. Neither can be treated as authoritative
      on its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Tax law outlives tenants.</strong> Indian GST rules require payment records to be kept for
      years after a restaurant stops being a customer, which means deleting a tenant cannot simply mean
      deleting its rows.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One machine, one process.</strong> Same shape as everything else here: the whole platform
      is a handful of containers on a single host.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="architecture">
    <MenuArchitecture />
    <div class="prose body-copy lifecycle">
      <p>
        Everything arrives at one reverse proxy, and a single explicit list of paths decides what
        belongs to the product. Anything not on that list falls through to a separate static
        marketing site. It is a blunt mechanism, and it is written down in one place rather than
        inferred from three.
      </p>
      <p>
        A menu request is served from an in-process cache when it is warm and from the database when
        it is not, then counted — asynchronously, so counting never delays the diner. Images go to
        object storage and are re-encoded on upload rather than served as uploaded.
      </p>
      <h3>The path that matters most</h3>
      <p>
        The request I care about is the one that follows a payment. It arrives twice, by two routes
        that know nothing about each other, and both of them are allowed to be first, late,
        duplicated, or absent. The design does not try to order them. It makes the outcome the same
        whichever way they land.
      </p>
    </div>
    <MenuPaymentFlow />
    <div class="prose body-copy lifecycle">
      <p>
        The activation function is the whole answer. It is safe to call twice because its first act
        is to check whether the payment is already recorded as captured — and if it is, to do
        nothing and say so. When it is not, the subscription change and the payment record are
        written in one transaction, so there is no window in which one exists without the other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="security">
    <p>
      A restaurant signs in with a short code and an eight-digit PIN. That is a small keyspace, so
      it is defended in layers: the PIN is combined with a server-held secret before hashing, which
      means a database taken on its own cannot be attacked offline; obvious PINs are rejected
      outright; and the login limit is applied per restaurant code rather than per address, so
      spreading an attack across many addresses buys nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Changing a PIN invalidates every existing session immediately. The token carries the time the
      PIN was last changed, and every request compares it against the current value, so revocation
      needs no session table to go and clear.
    </p>
    <p>
      Recovery is the part most likely to be abused, so it is the part most carefully built:
      identical responses whether or not an account exists, a one-time code stored only as a hash,
      attempts counted before the comparison rather than after it, and a short-lived single-use
      token to finish. Every step is written to the audit trail.
    </p>
    <p>
      On payments, the amount is decided by the server from the plan, never accepted from the
      browser. Signatures are compared in constant time. The webhook signature is checked against
      the exact bytes received, not against a re-serialised copy of the parsed body — re-serialising
      would change the bytes and break the comparison for reasons that would look like a gateway
      fault.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="operations">
    <p>
      Every push to the main branch rebuilds and redeploys the stack behind a health check, and a
      failing check fails the deploy rather than leaving a broken container running.
    </p>
    <p>
      The billing lifecycle runs as scheduled work inside the process, once an hour: trials that
      have expired, subscriptions that have lapsed into their grace window, grace windows that have
      run out, plan changes that were paid for and are now due, and reminder emails before a trial
      or a renewal date. Each job is wrapped separately so one failure cannot stop the rest.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same lifecycle is also applied lazily, on the way in: if an account's paid period lapsed
      since the last hourly tick, the correction is applied when they next sign in rather than
      making them wait for the clock. That means the state a restaurant sees is never stale, even
      between ticks.
    </p>
    <p>
      Backups are taken daily, encrypted before they leave the host, and pruned on a schedule. The
      restore path is a script with no default target, so restoring somewhere is always a deliberate
      act.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="outcomes">
    <p>
      No subscriber counts, revenue figures, or uptime percentages appear here. They exist, but not
      in any form I can point a reader at, and a number nobody can check is just a
      confident-sounding sentence. What follows is what the system is built to do.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="owner-input" set:html={ownerInputMarkers} />
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .lifecycle {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-8);
  }
</style>
